window.REQUETE_ENTRETIEN = window.REQUETE_ENTRETIEN || {};
window.REQUETE_ENTRETIEN["en"] = {"notions":{"Intégrité référentielle":"Referential integrity","Clé candidate":"Candidate key","Clé composite":"Composite key","Table d’association":"Junction table","Projection":"Projection","Index couvrant":"Covering index","Logique à trois valeurs":"Three-valued logic","Tri multicritère":"Multi-column sort","COUNT(colonne)":"COUNT(column)","Doublons":"Duplicates","Fuseau horaire":"Time zone","MIN() et MAX()":"MIN() and MAX()","Fonction d’agrégation":"Aggregate function","Jokers % et _":"Wildcards % and _","Collation":"Collation","Sous-requête":"Subquery","Bornes incluses":"Inclusive bounds","Dates":"Dates","Ordre des conditions":"Order of conditions","Alias de colonne":"Column alias","Alias de table":"Table alias","Ordre logique":"Logical order","Alias":"Alias","Anti-jointure":"Anti-join","Sous-requête corrélée":"Correlated subquery","Sous-requête scalaire":"Scalar subquery","Semi-jointure":"Semi-join","Division entière":"Integer division","Agrégat":"Aggregate","Dépendance fonctionnelle":"Functional dependency","Valeur par défaut":"Default value","Division par zéro":"Division by zero","B-tree":"B-tree","Coût des écritures":"Write cost","Index composite":"Composite index","Préfixe gauche":"Leftmost prefix","Ordre des colonnes":"Column order","Transaction":"Transaction","Atomicité":"Atomicity","Isolation":"Isolation","Autocommit":"Autocommit","Requête enregistrée":"Saved query","Droits d’accès":"Access rights","Redondance":"Redundancy","Formes normales":"Normal forms","Clé étrangère":"Foreign key","Hiérarchie":"Hierarchy","Agrégat conditionnel":"Conditional aggregate","Index":"Index","Sélectivité":"Selectivity","Verrou":"Lock","Interblocage":"Deadlock","Ordre d’accès":"Access order","Requête paramétrée":"Parameterized query","Injection SQL":"SQL injection","Moindre privilège":"Least privilege","Langage déclaratif":"Declarative language","Norme ISO":"ISO standard","Table":"Table","Clé primaire":"Primary key","Ligne":"Row","Colonne":"Column","Contrainte":"Constraint","Plan d’exécution":"Execution plan","Joker %":"Wildcard %","Joker _":"Wildcard _","Produit cartésien":"Cartesian product","Opérations ensemblistes":"Set operations","Types de données":"Data types","Conversion":"Conversion","excluded":"excluded","Rôle":"Role","schéma.table":"schema.table","Entrepôt de données":"Data warehouse","Table de faits":"Fact table","Dimensions":"Dimensions","Ordre d’exécution":"Order of execution","Table temporaire":"Temporary table","Session":"Session","strftime()":"strftime()","Schéma":"Schema","Jointure":"Join","Document":"Document","Chaîne vide":"Empty string","Dialecte SQL":"SQL dialect","Standard ISO":"ISO standard","Jointure externe":"Outer join","Granularité":"Granularity","Clé naturelle":"Natural key","Clé artificielle":"Surrogate key","Quartiles":"Quartiles","Dépendances":"Dependencies","Cohorte":"Cohort","Gaps and islands":"Gaps and islands","UPDATE … WHERE version":"UPDATE … WHERE version","Élagage":"Pruning","Clé de partitionnement":"Partition key","SCD type 2":"SCD type 2","Clé technique":"Surrogate key","date_debut / date_fin":"start_date / end_date","Table calendrier":"Calendar table","Pareto":"Pareto"},"questions":{"ent-v2-01":{"q":"What is a primary key?","a":"A primary key is the column that acts as an ID number for each row of a table, often an <code>id</code> column.\n\nA primary key follows two rules: its value is unique, so no two rows can share it, and it’s always filled in, never <code>NULL</code>. A table has only one primary key.\n\nI pick a value that never changes, usually a number the database assigns automatically. Other tables use it to point to one specific row.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE t (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL\n);"},"ent-v2-02":{"q":"What is a foreign key?","a":"A foreign key is a column that refers to the primary key of another table. It’s what creates the link between the two tables.\n\nThe database checks this link: it rejects a value that matches no row in the other table, and it prevents deleting a row that’s still used elsewhere.\n\nI use it to link data without copying it: each piece of information is written only once, and a join brings it together when reading.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE child (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  parent_id INTEGER\n    REFERENCES parent (id)\n);"},"ent-v2-03":{"q":"What’s the difference between PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE?","a":"Both forbid duplicates. The difference: a table has only one primary key, always filled in, while it can have several <code>UNIQUE</code> columns, which accept empty values (<code>NULL</code>).\n\nThe primary key identifies each row. <code>UNIQUE</code> protects other columns that must not repeat, like an email address.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE t (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  email TEXT UNIQUE\n);"},"ent-v2-04":{"q":"What is a composite key?","a":"A composite key is a key made of several columns. It’s their combination that must be unique, not each column on its own.\n\nSo the same value can appear several times in one column. The database only rejects a new row that repeats exactly the same combination of values as an existing row.\n\nI mostly see it in tables that link two other tables: the key then reuses the primary key of each of the two tables.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE t (\n  a INTEGER,\n  b INTEGER,\n  PRIMARY KEY (a, b)\n);"},"ent-v2-05":{"q":"What’s the difference between SELECT * and SELECT col1, col2?","a":"<code>SELECT *</code> returns every column of the table. <code>SELECT col1, col2</code> returns only the columns I name, in the order I write them.\n\n<code>SELECT *</code> is handy for exploring a table. In a real query, I name the columns: the result is clearer, lighter, and it doesn’t change if someone adds a column to the table.","syntaxe":"-- every column\nSELECT * FROM t;\n\n-- only the useful ones\nSELECT a, b FROM t;"},"ent-v2-06":{"q":"What’s the difference between WHERE and HAVING?","a":"<code>WHERE</code> filters rows before grouping; <code>HAVING</code> filters groups, after the <code>GROUP BY</code>.\n\nSo <code>WHERE</code> doesn’t know about totals or counts like <code>COUNT(*)</code> yet. A condition on a column goes in <code>WHERE</code>; a condition on a total or a count goes in <code>HAVING</code>.\n\nWhen a condition could go in either, I put it in <code>WHERE</code>: the database works on fewer rows, and the query runs faster.","syntaxe":"SELECT a, COUNT(*)\nFROM t\nWHERE b = 'x'  -- the rows\nGROUP BY a\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n-- HAVING: the groups"},"ent-v2-07":{"q":"How do you check whether a value is NULL?","a":"I write <code>IS NULL</code> or <code>IS NOT NULL</code>. <code>NULL</code> means the value is missing or unknown.\n\nThe trap: <code>= NULL</code> doesn’t work. The query gives no error, but it returns no rows, because an unknown value equals nothing, not even another unknown value.","syntaxe":"SELECT *\nFROM t\nWHERE col IS NULL;\n\n-- the opposite\nWHERE col IS NOT NULL"},"ent-v2-08":{"q":"What is ORDER BY for?","a":"<code>ORDER BY</code> sorts the result rows: <code>ASC</code> from smallest to largest (the default), <code>DESC</code> from largest to smallest.\n\nI can sort on several columns: the second one only breaks ties in the first. I set <code>ASC</code> or <code>DESC</code> separately for each column.\n\nWithout <code>ORDER BY</code>, the order of the rows is never guaranteed, even if it always looks the same.\n\nCommon follow-up: what if the sort column isn’t in the <code>SELECT</code>? The sort usually still works.","syntaxe":"SELECT a, b\nFROM t\nORDER BY a ASC, b DESC;"},"ent-v2-09":{"q":"How do you limit the number of rows returned?","a":"I add a limit to the query: <code>LIMIT 10</code> keeps only the first 10 rows. SQL Server writes <code>TOP 10</code> right after <code>SELECT</code>.\n\nThe limit applies after sorting. With a descending <code>ORDER BY</code> and <code>LIMIT 3</code>, I get the three largest values. Without <code>ORDER BY</code>, I get three arbitrary rows, which can change from one run to the next.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite\nSELECT * FROM t\nORDER BY a\nLIMIT 10;\n\n-- SQL Server\nSELECT TOP 10 * FROM t\nORDER BY a;"},"ent-v2-10":{"q":"What’s the difference between COUNT(*), COUNT(column) and COUNT(DISTINCT column)?","a":"<code>COUNT(*)</code> counts every row. <code>COUNT(col)</code> counts only the rows where that column is filled in. <code>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code> counts the different values, each one only once.\n\nSo on the same table, the three results can differ: empty cells and duplicates don’t count everywhere.","syntaxe":"SELECT\n  COUNT(*),\n  COUNT(col),\n  COUNT(DISTINCT col)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-11":{"q":"What’s the difference between INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN?","a":"<code>INNER JOIN</code> keeps only the rows that have a match in both tables. <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps every row of the first table, even the ones with no match.\n\nWhen a row of the first table has nothing to match, <code>INNER JOIN</code> drops it. <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps it, with empty cells (<code>NULL</code>) for the columns of the second table.","syntaxe":"SELECT t1.a, t2.b\nFROM t1\nLEFT JOIN t2\n  ON t2.t1_id = t1.id;\n-- INNER JOIN: only\n-- the linked rows"},"ent-v2-12":{"q":"What is DISTINCT for?","a":"<code>DISTINCT</code> removes duplicate rows from the result: each row appears only once.\n\nThe <code>DISTINCT</code> clause applies to all the requested columns, taken together. With one column, I get the list of its different values. With two columns, I get each different combination.\n\n<code>DISTINCT</code> doesn’t change the table: it only changes what the query shows.","syntaxe":"SELECT DISTINCT a, b\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-13":{"q":"How do you get today’s date?","a":"<code>CURRENT_DATE</code> gives today’s date, and <code>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code> the date with the time. That’s how the SQL standard writes them.\n\nEach database system also has its own function, like <code>NOW()</code> or <code>GETDATE()</code>.\n\nWatch out for columns that also store the time: a value from today at 2 p.m. isn’t equal to today’s date on its own.","syntaxe":"SELECT CURRENT_DATE;\n-- SQL Server:\nSELECT CAST(GETDATE()\n  AS DATE);"},"ent-v2-14":{"q":"How do you concatenate several strings?","a":"Concatenating means joining several texts end to end. I use <code>CONCAT()</code>, which exists almost everywhere, or the SQL standard <code>||</code> operator.\n\nTo separate the pieces, I add a space between them, for example between a first name and a last name.\n\nWatch out for empty values: with <code>||</code>, a single <code>NULL</code> piece makes the whole result <code>NULL</code>.","syntaxe":"-- standard (PostgreSQL,\n-- SQLite, Oracle)\nSELECT a || ' ' || b\nFROM t;\n\n-- MySQL, SQL Server\nSELECT CONCAT(a, ' ', b)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-15":{"q":"What are SUM(), AVG(), MIN() and MAX() for?","a":"They’re calculation functions: they summarize several rows into a single value.\n\n<code>SUM()</code> adds up, <code>AVG()</code> averages, <code>MIN()</code> gives the smallest value and <code>MAX()</code> the largest.\n\nAll four ignore empty cells (<code>NULL</code>). So the average is calculated only on the filled-in values.","syntaxe":"SELECT\n  SUM(amount),\n  AVG(amount),\n  MIN(amount),\n  MAX(amount)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-16":{"q":"What is GROUP BY for?","a":"<code>GROUP BY</code> gathers the rows that have the same value in a column, to calculate something per group: a total per category, a number of rows per month.\n\nThe query then returns one row per group. In the <code>SELECT</code>, I put the columns written in the <code>GROUP BY</code>, and calculations like <code>COUNT(*)</code> or <code>SUM()</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT category,\n  COUNT(*) AS n,\n  SUM(amount) AS total\nFROM t\nGROUP BY category;"},"ent-v2-17":{"q":"What’s the difference between = and LIKE?","a":"<code>=</code> looks for an exact value. <code>LIKE</code> looks for text that matches a pattern.\n\nIn that pattern, <code>%</code> stands for any sequence of characters. So <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> finds every text that starts with “abc”.\n\nWhen I know the exact value, I use <code>=</code>: it’s clearer and faster.","syntaxe":"WHERE name = 'abc'\nWHERE name LIKE 'ab%'"},"ent-v2-18":{"q":"What is IN for?","a":"<code>IN</code> checks whether a value is in a list. It’s a short way to write several <code>=</code> joined by <code>OR</code>.\n\nThe list can be written by hand, or come from another query. <code>NOT IN</code>, on the other hand, keeps the values that aren’t in the list.","syntaxe":"SELECT *\nFROM t\nWHERE a IN (1, 2, 3);"},"ent-v2-19":{"q":"What is BETWEEN for?","a":"<code>BETWEEN</code> checks whether a value lies between two bounds. Both bounds are included: <code>BETWEEN 10 AND 20</code> keeps 10 and 20.\n\n<code>BETWEEN</code> works with numbers, dates and texts.\n\nCareful with dates that have a time: the end bound stops at exactly midnight, at the very start of the last day. So the rest of that day is left out. For a whole month, I’d rather write <code>&gt;=</code> the 1st of the month and <code>&lt;</code> the 1st of the next month.","syntaxe":"SELECT *\nFROM t\nWHERE a BETWEEN 10 AND 20;\n-- bounds included"},"ent-v2-20":{"q":"What is CASE WHEN for?","a":"<code>CASE WHEN</code> lets you write an “if… then… else” in a query. For each row, it returns a value based on a condition.\n\nThe conditions are read in order, and the first true one wins. <code>ELSE</code> gives the value when no condition is true; without <code>ELSE</code>, the result is empty (<code>NULL</code>).\n\nI often use it to sort values into categories, like “low”, “medium” or “high”.","syntaxe":"SELECT a,\n  CASE\n    WHEN a > 100 THEN 'high'\n    WHEN a > 10 THEN 'medium'\n    ELSE 'low'\n  END AS tier\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-21":{"q":"What’s the difference between DELETE, TRUNCATE and DROP?","a":"<code>DELETE</code> removes rows: all of them, or only the ones chosen with <code>WHERE</code>. <code>TRUNCATE</code> empties the whole table at once. <code>DROP TABLE</code> removes the entire table, along with its structure.\n\nAfter a <code>DELETE</code> or a <code>TRUNCATE</code>, the table still exists, empty or not. After a <code>DROP</code>, it no longer exists.\n\n<code>TRUNCATE</code> is faster than <code>DELETE</code> on a large table, but it doesn’t let you choose the rows.","syntaxe":"DELETE FROM t WHERE a = 1;\nTRUNCATE TABLE t;\nDROP TABLE t;"},"ent-v2-22":{"q":"What are the main SQL constraints?","a":"A constraint is a rule the database checks on every insert or update. If the rule isn’t met, the row is rejected.\n\nThe main ones: <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> identifies each row, <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> links to another table, <code>UNIQUE</code> forbids duplicates, <code>NOT NULL</code> requires a value, and <code>CHECK</code> enforces a condition, like a positive price.\n\nThese constraints protect the data, whatever application writes it.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE t (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  code TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  qty INTEGER DEFAULT 0\n    CHECK (qty >= 0),\n  p_id INTEGER\n    REFERENCES p (id)\n);"},"ent-v2-23":{"q":"What’s the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?","a":"Both put the results of two queries end to end. <code>UNION</code> removes duplicates; <code>UNION ALL</code> keeps every row.\n\n<code>UNION ALL</code> is faster, because it doesn’t have to look for duplicates. I use it by default, and I only pick <code>UNION</code> when I really want to remove duplicates.\n\nBoth queries must have the same number of columns, in the same order.","syntaxe":"SELECT a FROM t1\nUNION       -- no duplicates\nSELECT a FROM t2;\n\nSELECT a FROM t1\nUNION ALL   -- keep everything\nSELECT a FROM t2;"},"ent-v2-24":{"q":"What is an alias with AS for?","a":"An alias gives a temporary name to a column or a table, just for the query. Nothing is renamed in the database.\n\nOn a column, it gives a clear name to a calculation, like “total” or “average”. On a table, it shortens the writing: I can write <code>t.col</code> instead of the full name.","syntaxe":"SELECT a AS label,\n  SUM(b) AS total\nFROM t AS x;"},"ent-v2-25":{"q":"In what logical order is a SELECT query executed?","a":"The database doesn’t read the query in the order I write it. It starts with <code>FROM</code> (the tables), then <code>WHERE</code> (the rows), <code>GROUP BY</code> (the groups), <code>HAVING</code> (the groups kept), and only then <code>SELECT</code> (the columns), and finally <code>ORDER BY</code> (the sort) and the limit.\n\nThat’s why most databases reject, in the <code>WHERE</code>, an alias created in the <code>SELECT</code>: that alias doesn’t exist yet. SQLite is the exception and accepts it. The alias can, however, be used in <code>ORDER BY</code>.","syntaxe":"-- writing order\nSELECT … FROM … WHERE …\nGROUP BY … HAVING …\nORDER BY … LIMIT …\n-- logical order:\n-- FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY,\n-- HAVING, SELECT,\n-- ORDER BY, LIMIT"},"ent-v2-26":{"q":"What are the main types of joins?","a":"A join brings two tables together through a shared column. The main types are <code>INNER JOIN</code>, <code>LEFT JOIN</code>, <code>RIGHT JOIN</code>, <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> and <code>CROSS JOIN</code>.\n\n<code>INNER JOIN</code> keeps only the rows that have a match in both tables. <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps every row of the left table (the one written before <code>JOIN</code>), <code>RIGHT JOIN</code> every row of the right one, and <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> every row of both: when nothing matches, the other table’s columns are empty (<code>NULL</code>).\n\n<code>CROSS JOIN</code> pairs each row of one table with each row of the other, with no condition. Joining a table to itself (a self join) isn’t a separate type: it’s one of these joins, with two aliases.","syntaxe":"FROM t1 INNER JOIN t2 ON …\nFROM t1 LEFT JOIN t2 ON …\nFROM t1 RIGHT JOIN t2 ON …\nFROM t1 FULL JOIN t2 ON …\nFROM t1 CROSS JOIN t2"},"ent-v2-27":{"q":"How do you find rows with no match in another table?","a":"I look for the rows of one table that have nothing matching in the other. Two classic ways: a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> followed by <code>WHERE … IS NULL</code>, or <code>NOT EXISTS</code>.\n\nThe <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps every row of the first table; when nothing matches, the second table’s columns are empty. I then keep only those rows, with <code>IS NULL</code>. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> says it directly: “there is no linked row”.\n\nI avoid <code>NOT IN</code>: if the other table contains a <code>NULL</code> value, the query no longer returns anything.","syntaxe":"SELECT t1.*\nFROM t1\nLEFT JOIN t2\n  ON t2.t1_id = t1.id\nWHERE t2.id IS NULL;"},"ent-v2-28":{"q":"What’s the difference between a correlated and a non-correlated subquery?","a":"A non-correlated subquery is independent: I could run it on its own, and its result is the same for every row. A correlated subquery uses a column from the main query: its result changes with each row.\n\nComparing each value with the overall average takes a non-correlated subquery, calculated only once. Comparing each value with the average of its own group takes a correlated subquery, which picks up the group of the current row.\n\nA correlated subquery can be slower, because in principle it’s recalculated for each row. A window function or a join often does the same job faster.","syntaxe":"-- non-correlated\nWHERE a > (SELECT AVG(a)\n           FROM t)\n\n-- correlated\nWHERE a > (SELECT AVG(a)\n  FROM t AS u\n  WHERE u.g = t.g)"},"ent-v2-29":{"q":"What’s the difference between IN and EXISTS?","a":"<code>IN</code> checks whether a value is in a list. <code>EXISTS</code> only checks whether a subquery finds at least one row, without looking at what it contains.\n\nWith <code>EXISTS</code>, the subquery is usually tied to the current row, and it stops as soon as it finds a row.\n\nThe real difference shows up with negation: if the list contains a <code>NULL</code>, <code>NOT IN</code> no longer returns anything, while <code>NOT EXISTS</code> works normally. Speed-wise, modern databases often handle them the same way: I pick the more readable one.","syntaxe":"WHERE id IN (\n  SELECT t1_id FROM t2)\n\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM t2\n  WHERE t2.t1_id = t1.id)"},"ent-v2-30":{"q":"What are ANY and ALL for?","a":"<code>ANY</code> and <code>ALL</code> compare a value with all the values of a subquery. With <code>ANY</code>, the comparison only has to be true for one value; with <code>ALL</code>, it must be true for all of them.\n\nSo <code>&gt; ANY</code> means “greater than at least one value”, in other words greater than the smallest. <code>&gt; ALL</code> means “greater than all of them”, in other words greater than the largest. <code>= ANY</code> is the same as <code>IN</code>.\n\nI rarely see them in practice: <code>MIN()</code>, <code>MAX()</code>, <code>IN</code> or <code>EXISTS</code> often say the same thing more clearly. SQLite doesn’t support them.","syntaxe":"WHERE a > ANY (\n  SELECT b FROM t2)\n-- greater than at least one\n\nWHERE a > ALL (\n  SELECT b FROM t2)\n-- greater than all"},"ent-v2-31":{"q":"What is a CTE?","a":"A CTE (for “Common Table Expression”) is an intermediate query I give a name with <code>WITH</code>, at the very beginning. The rest of the query then uses it like a table.\n\nA CTE helps break a complicated query into readable steps: a first step calculates totals, the main query filters them or links them to other tables. I can chain several CTEs, separated by commas.\n\nThe CTE exists only during the query: nothing is saved in the database.","syntaxe":"WITH summary AS (\n  SELECT g, SUM(v) AS total\n  FROM t\n  GROUP BY g\n)\nSELECT *\nFROM summary\nWHERE total > 100;"},"ent-v2-32":{"q":"How do you calculate a percentage per group?","a":"I divide each group’s value by the total of all groups, then multiply by 100.\n\nTo get that total on every row without writing a second query, I use a window function, meaning a calculation over several rows that doesn’t group them. In <code>SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER ()</code>, <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts each group, then <code>SUM(…) OVER ()</code> adds up all those counts and copies the total onto every row.\n\nI multiply by <code>100.0</code> rather than <code>100</code>: between two whole numbers, some databases do integer division, with no decimals, and the result can drop to 0. For a share within a larger group, I add <code>PARTITION BY</code> in the <code>OVER</code>: the total is then calculated separately for each larger group.","syntaxe":"SELECT g,\n  100.0 * COUNT(*)\n  / SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER ()\n  AS pct\nFROM t\nGROUP BY g;"},"ent-v2-33":{"q":"Can you select a non-aggregated column with GROUP BY?","a":"Generally, no. The result has only one row per group: so every column in the <code>SELECT</code> must be in the <code>GROUP BY</code>, or in a calculation like <code>COUNT()</code>, <code>MIN()</code> or <code>MAX()</code>.\n\nA column that has several values within the same group can’t be shown as is: the database doesn’t know which one to pick. I put it in a calculation, or I add it to the <code>GROUP BY</code>.\n\nCareful: SQLite, and MySQL with some settings, still accept the query and pick an arbitrary value. The result then can’t be trusted.\n\nCommon follow-up: what if I group by the primary key? The table’s other columns then have only one value per group. PostgreSQL and MySQL let you show them; SQL Server and Oracle require adding them to the <code>GROUP BY</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT g, MAX(b) AS b\nFROM t\nGROUP BY g;\n-- b neither grouped\n-- nor aggregated: rejected"},"ent-v2-34":{"q":"What is COALESCE for?","a":"<code>COALESCE</code> returns the first value in its list that isn’t empty (<code>NULL</code>), read from left to right.\n\n<code>COALESCE</code> replaces a missing value: with another column, or with a default value, like 0 or “Unknown”. In a calculation, <code>COALESCE</code> stops a single empty cell from making the whole result empty.\n\n<code>COALESCE</code> is part of the SQL standard: the function works in every database. Some databases also have their own version, limited to two values, like <code>IFNULL</code>, <code>ISNULL</code> or <code>NVL</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT COALESCE(a, b, 0)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-35":{"q":"What is NULLIF for?","a":"<code>NULLIF(a, b)</code> returns <code>NULL</code> if the two values are equal, and the first value otherwise.\n\nIts most common use: avoiding division by zero. With <code>x / NULLIF(y, 0)</code>, a zero becomes <code>NULL</code>, and the division gives <code>NULL</code> instead of an error.\n\n<code>NULLIF</code> also turns an empty text into <code>NULL</code>, so empty cells and empty texts are handled the same way. If I’d rather show 0, I wrap the calculation in a <code>COALESCE</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT total\n  / NULLIF(n, 0)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-36":{"q":"How do you find duplicates?","a":"I group the rows by the columns that should be unique, then keep the groups that have more than one row, with <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>.\n\nThat gives me the duplicated values and how many there are. To see the rows themselves, I reuse this result in a subquery, or add the count on every row with a window function.\n\nFirst of all, I define what a duplicate is: on which columns, and whether to take capital letters and extra spaces into account (with <code>LOWER(TRIM(col))</code>).","syntaxe":"SELECT a, b, COUNT(*)\nFROM t\nGROUP BY a, b\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;"},"ent-v2-37":{"q":"How do you delete duplicates while keeping one row?","a":"I number the rows of each group of duplicates with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, then delete every row whose number is above 1.\n\nIn the <code>OVER</code> of <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, <code>PARTITION BY</code> creates a group per duplicated value. <code>ORDER BY</code> chooses the row that gets number 1, so the one I keep: the oldest, the newest or the most complete.\n\nTo be safe, I first run the selection on its own to check the targeted rows. Then I delete inside a transaction, which I can undo if something goes wrong. Finally, a <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint stops the duplicates from coming back.","syntaxe":"WITH n AS (\n  SELECT id,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY a, b\n      ORDER BY id) AS rn\n  FROM t\n)\nDELETE FROM t\nWHERE id IN (\n  SELECT id FROM n\n  WHERE rn > 1);"},"ent-v2-38":{"q":"What are window functions for?","a":"A window function calculates over a group of rows while keeping every row in the result. That’s the difference with <code>GROUP BY</code>, which reduces each group to a single row.\n\nI write it with <code>OVER</code>: <code>PARTITION BY</code> defines the group, and <code>ORDER BY</code> the order of the rows within that group. A window function is used to rank rows, read the previous row, compute a running total, or compare a row with its group’s total.\n\nThe window function is calculated after the <code>WHERE</code>: to filter on its result, I go through a CTE or a subquery.","syntaxe":"SELECT a,\n  func() OVER (\n    PARTITION BY g\n    ORDER BY d\n  )\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-39":{"q":"What’s the difference between GROUP BY and PARTITION BY?","a":"<code>GROUP BY</code> groups the rows: the result contains only one row per group. <code>PARTITION BY</code>, written inside <code>OVER</code>, also forms groups, but for a window function: every row stays.\n\n<code>GROUP BY</code> answers “what is each group’s average?”. <code>PARTITION BY</code> answers “how does each row compare with its group’s average?”, because the average is shown next to each row.\n\nThe two can be combined in the same query: the window function is calculated after the <code>GROUP BY</code>.","syntaxe":"-- one row per group\nSELECT g, SUM(v)\nFROM t GROUP BY g;\n\n-- every row\nSELECT g, v,\n  SUM(v) OVER (\n    PARTITION BY g)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-40":{"q":"What’s the difference between ROW_NUMBER(), RANK() and DENSE_RANK()?","a":"All three number the rows in the chosen order. These three functions differ only for ties.\n\n<code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> always gives a different number: 1, 2, 3, 4. <code>RANK()</code> gives tied rows the same rank, then skips numbers: 1, 2, 2, 4. <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> also gives the same rank, but without skipping: 1, 2, 2, 3.\n\nI choose based on the need: <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> to keep exactly one row per group, <code>RANK()</code> for a competition ranking, <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> for the top n distinct values.","syntaxe":"SELECT v,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER w,\n  RANK() OVER w,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER w\nFROM t\nWINDOW w AS\n  (ORDER BY v DESC);"},"ent-v2-41":{"q":"What are LAG() and LEAD() for?","a":"<code>LAG()</code> reads a value from the previous row, and <code>LEAD()</code> from the next row, in the order I choose. That way I compare a row with its neighbor, without a join.\n\n<code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER</code> defines what the “previous” row is, and <code>PARTITION BY</code> limits the search to the same group. When there’s no row before or after, the result is <code>NULL</code>.\n\nI use them to calculate a change from one month to the next, or the time elapsed between two events.","syntaxe":"SELECT d, v,\n  LAG(v) OVER (\n    ORDER BY d) AS prev,\n  LEAD(v) OVER (\n    ORDER BY d) AS next\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-42":{"q":"How do you calculate a running total?","a":"I use <code>SUM()</code> as a window function, with an <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER</code>: each row gets the sum of all the rows before it, itself included.\n\n<code>PARTITION BY</code> restarts the running total from zero for each group, for example for each customer.\n\nWatch out for ties: by default, two rows with the same sort value get the same running total. To really add up row by row, I add <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> inside the <code>OVER</code>, which means “from the first row to the current row”.","syntaxe":"SELECT d, v,\n  SUM(v) OVER (\n    ORDER BY d\n    ROWS UNBOUNDED\n      PRECEDING\n  ) AS running_total\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-43":{"q":"What is an index?","a":"An index is like the index at the back of a book: it tells where the rows containing a value are, without having to read the whole table.\n\nThe values are stored in order in it. So an index speeds up searches, but also sorts and joins on that column.\n\nIn return, it takes up space, and every insert, update or delete has to update it, which slows down writes. So I only index the columns often used in filters, joins and sorts.","syntaxe":"CREATE INDEX idx_t_a\n  ON t (a);"},"ent-v2-44":{"q":"What is a composite index?","a":"A composite index covers several columns, in a specific order: an index on (a, b) is sorted by a, then by b.\n\nIt’s like a phone book sorted by last name, then by first name: it quickly finds everyone with the same last name, or one specific person, but not everyone with the same first name. So the index helps searches on a, alone or with b, but usually not on b alone.\n\nThe order of the columns matters: I put first the ones I test with <code>=</code>, then the one I test on a range (with <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code> or <code>BETWEEN</code>) or the one used for sorting.","syntaxe":"CREATE INDEX idx_t_a_b\n  ON t (a, b);\n-- helps: WHERE a = …\n-- and WHERE a = … AND b = …"},"ent-v2-45":{"q":"What does ACID mean?","a":"ACID stands for the four guarantees of a transaction, meaning a group of changes handled as a single block: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability.\n\nAtomicity: everything is done, or nothing. Consistency: at the end, the database respects all its rules. Isolation: two transactions running at the same time don’t get in each other’s way. Durability: once committed, the changes survive even a crash.\n\nClassic example: a bank transfer takes money out of one account and adds it to another. Thanks to atomicity, both operations succeed together, or neither is done."},"ent-v2-46":{"q":"What’s the difference between COMMIT and ROLLBACK?","a":"<code>COMMIT</code> confirms a transaction: its changes become permanent and visible to other users. <code>ROLLBACK</code> cancels it: everything it changed is undone.\n\nBefore the <code>COMMIT</code>, everything is provisional. After it, there’s no going back.\n\nBy default, most databases commit each statement on its own. To group several statements, I start a transaction with <code>BEGIN</code>, then end it with <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>.","syntaxe":"BEGIN;\nUPDATE t SET a = 1\n  WHERE id = 1;\nCOMMIT;   -- or ROLLBACK;"},"ent-v2-47":{"q":"What is a SAVEPOINT for?","a":"A <code>SAVEPOINT</code> sets a return point inside a transaction. <code>ROLLBACK TO</code> undoes only what was done since that point: the rest is kept, and the transaction goes on.\n\nI use it in a multi-step process: if a step fails, I go back to the point set just before it, without losing the steps that already worked. The final <code>COMMIT</code> confirms everything.\n\nSQL Server writes it <code>SAVE TRANSACTION</code>.","syntaxe":"BEGIN;\nINSERT INTO t …;\nSAVEPOINT step1;\nUPDATE t …;\nROLLBACK TO step1;\nCOMMIT;"},"ent-v2-48":{"q":"What is a SQL view?","a":"A view is a query saved under a name, which I can query like a table. A view stores no data: every time it’s read, the database reruns its query on the original tables.\n\nA view simplifies complicated queries, avoids repeating the same joins and gives a clear name to a business rule.\n\nA view also helps with security: I can grant access to the view without granting access to the table, and so show only some rows or columns. On the other hand, a view doesn’t speed up queries: to keep an already calculated result, you need a materialized view.","syntaxe":"CREATE VIEW v AS\nSELECT a, b\nFROM t\nWHERE c = 1;\n\nSELECT * FROM v;"},"ent-v2-49":{"q":"What is database normalization?","a":"Normalization organizes data into several linked tables, so that each piece of information is stored only once.\n\nIf an address is copied onto a hundred rows, all hundred rows must be updated when it changes, and missing one leaves two different versions. Once normalized, the address lives in a single table, and the other tables refer to it with a key.\n\nIn practice, I aim for the third normal form. Concretely: one value per cell, and each column depends on the whole key of its table, and on nothing else. For example, a product’s name goes in the products table, not in the orders table.\n\nFor analysis, I sometimes denormalize on purpose: I copy some data to avoid joins."},"ent-v2-50":{"q":"What’s the difference between a 1:N and an N:N relationship?","a":"In a 1:N relationship, a row of one table can be linked to several rows of another, but each of those rows has only one link back: a customer has several orders, an order has a single customer. I create it with a foreign key, placed on the N side.\n\nIn an N:N relationship, the links go both ways: a student takes several courses, and a course has several students. A foreign key is no longer enough: I add a junction table, which holds both keys.\n\nThat table’s primary key is often the combination of the two keys, which prevents saving the same link twice.","syntaxe":"-- 1:N\nCREATE TABLE child (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  parent_id INTEGER\n    REFERENCES parent (id)\n);\n-- N:N: junction table\nCREATE TABLE a_b (\n  a_id INTEGER\n    REFERENCES a (id),\n  b_id INTEGER\n    REFERENCES b (id),\n  PRIMARY KEY (a_id, b_id)\n);"},"ent-v2-51":{"q":"What’s the difference between ROWS and RANGE in a window function?","a":"<code>ROWS</code> and <code>RANGE</code> define which rows a window function takes into account around the current row. This set of rows is called the frame of the calculation.\n\n<code>ROWS</code> counts rows: “the 2 previous rows” means exactly two rows. <code>RANGE</code> works on the values of the sort column: rows that have the same value are always taken together.\n\nThe difference shows with ties. In a running total, <code>ROWS</code> adds one row at a time, while <code>RANGE</code> gives the same running total to every row with the same value. Careful: with an <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER</code> and no frame specified, <code>RANGE</code> applies, from the start of the group to the current row.","syntaxe":"SUM(v) OVER (ORDER BY d\n  ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING\n  AND CURRENT ROW)\n\nSUM(v) OVER (ORDER BY d\n  RANGE BETWEEN\n  UNBOUNDED PRECEDING\n  AND CURRENT ROW)"},"ent-v2-52":{"q":"How do you get the top 3 results in each group?","a":"I rank the rows of each group with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, then keep the ones whose rank is 3 or less.\n\n<code>PARTITION BY</code> makes a separate ranking for each group, and <code>ORDER BY</code> sets the order, from best to worst. A window function can’t be used directly in the <code>WHERE</code>. So I put this ranking in a CTE, meaning a named subquery written with <code>WITH</code>, then filter on the rank in the main query.\n\nTo keep ties, I’d use <code>RANK()</code> or <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> instead.","syntaxe":"WITH r AS (\n  SELECT *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY g\n      ORDER BY v DESC) AS n\n  FROM t\n)\nSELECT *\nFROM r\nWHERE n <= 3;"},"ent-v2-53":{"q":"What are FIRST_VALUE() and LAST_VALUE() for?","a":"<code>FIRST_VALUE()</code> returns the first value of a group of rows, and <code>LAST_VALUE()</code> the last one, in the chosen order. These two functions compare each row with a reference value, like the first or the last known value.\n\nThe trap with <code>LAST_VALUE()</code>: by default, the calculation only looks at the rows from the start of the group to the current row. So the “last” value is usually the current row’s, not the one at the end of the group. To get the real last value, I extend the calculation to the whole group with <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</code>.\n\nA simpler trick: <code>FIRST_VALUE()</code> with the order reversed.","syntaxe":"SELECT g, d, v,\n  FIRST_VALUE(v) OVER w,\n  LAST_VALUE(v) OVER w\nFROM t\nWINDOW w AS (\n  PARTITION BY g\n  ORDER BY d\n  ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED\n  PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED\n  FOLLOWING);"},"ent-v2-54":{"q":"What is a recursive CTE?","a":"A CTE is a subquery I give a name with <code>WITH</code>. A recursive CTE is a CTE that calls itself. This kind of CTE is used to walk through data organized as a tree: an org chart, categories and subcategories.\n\nThe query has two parts, joined by <code>UNION ALL</code>: a starting point (for example the top of the hierarchy), then a part that adds the next level on each round. The rounds stop when no new row is found.\n\nThe trap: if the data forms a loop, the query never ends. So I limit the number of levels.","syntaxe":"WITH RECURSIVE h AS (\n  SELECT id, parent_id\n  FROM t\n  WHERE parent_id IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT t.id, t.parent_id\n  FROM t\n  JOIN h\n    ON t.parent_id = h.id\n)\nSELECT * FROM h;"},"ent-v2-55":{"q":"Why can NOT IN be dangerous with NULL?","a":"If the subquery of a <code>NOT IN</code> contains a single <code>NULL</code>, the query no longer returns any row, with no error.\n\nThe reason: “3 is not in (1, 2, NULL)” amounts to saying “3 is different from 1, from 2 and from NULL”. But comparing with <code>NULL</code> gives “unknown”, never true: so the whole condition is never true.\n\nI prefer <code>NOT EXISTS</code>, which doesn’t have this problem, or a <code>LEFT JOIN … IS NULL</code>. <code>NOT IN</code> stays safe if the column can’t contain <code>NULL</code>.","syntaxe":"-- dangerous if b has a NULL\nWHERE a NOT IN (\n  SELECT b FROM t2)\n\n-- safe\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM t2\n  WHERE t2.b = t1.a)"},"ent-v2-56":{"q":"How do you pivot without a PIVOT operator?","a":"A pivot turns rows into columns: for example one column per month instead of one row per month.\n\nWithout a <code>PIVOT</code> operator, I use a conditional calculation: <code>SUM(CASE WHEN … THEN value END)</code> for each column I want, with a <code>GROUP BY</code>. Each <code>CASE WHEN</code> keeps only the values of one category.\n\nThis method works in every DBMS, and I can mix calculations (sum, count, average) in the same query.","syntaxe":"SELECT g,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN m = 1\n      THEN v ELSE 0 END)\n    AS m1,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN m = 2\n      THEN v ELSE 0 END)\n    AS m2\nFROM t\nGROUP BY g;"},"ent-v2-57":{"q":"How do you simplify a conditional aggregation in PostgreSQL?","a":"PostgreSQL offers <code>FILTER (WHERE …)</code>, written right after a calculation like <code>SUM()</code> or <code>COUNT()</code>. Only the rows that meet the condition go into the calculation.\n\nIt’s a more readable way to write <code>SUM(CASE WHEN … THEN … END)</code>. That way I calculate several metrics in the same query, each with its own condition.\n\n<code>FILTER</code> is part of the SQL standard and also exists in SQLite. In MySQL and SQL Server, I stick with <code>CASE WHEN</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT g,\n  COUNT(*) FILTER (\n    WHERE ok) AS n_ok,\n  SUM(v) FILTER (\n    WHERE m = 1) AS m1\nFROM t\nGROUP BY g;"},"ent-v2-58":{"q":"How do you pivot with SQL Server?","a":"SQL Server offers the <code>PIVOT</code> operator, which turns the values of a column into several columns, with a calculation like <code>SUM()</code>.\n\n<code>FOR col</code> gives the column whose values become columns, and <code>IN (…)</code> the list of those columns. This list must be written in advance. If the values change often, I build the query text in code, with the up-to-date list, before running it.\n\nThe other method, with <code>SUM(CASE WHEN …)</code>, works everywhere and is often simpler to read.","syntaxe":"SELECT g, [1], [2]\nFROM (\n  SELECT g, m, v FROM t\n) AS s\nPIVOT (\n  SUM(v) FOR m IN ([1], [2])\n) AS p;"},"ent-v2-59":{"q":"How do you query JSONB data in PostgreSQL?","a":"JSON is a text format that stores information as “key: value” pairs, sometimes nested. In PostgreSQL, the <code>JSONB</code> type stores these documents in a form that’s fast to query.\n\nI use <code>-&gt;</code> to extract a JSON value, <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> to get it as text, and <code>@&gt;</code> to check that a document contains a specific structure or values. I chain the arrows to go down into a nested object.\n\nTo speed up searches over many rows, I add a GIN index on the column: this kind of index can search inside documents. I prefer <code>JSONB</code> to <code>JSON</code>, which just keeps the original text and is slower to search.","syntaxe":"SELECT\n  data ->> 'name',\n  data -> 'address'\n       ->> 'city'\nFROM t\nWHERE data @> '{\"ok\":true}';"},"ent-v2-60":{"q":"How do you query a JSON column in MySQL?","a":"In MySQL, <code>JSON_EXTRACT()</code> reads a value in a JSON column, from a path like <code>$.key</code>: <code>$</code> stands for the start of the document.\n\nThere are two shortcuts: <code>-&gt;</code> returns the value in JSON format, with quotes for a text, and <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> returns the bare text, without quotes. To show or compare a value, I usually use <code>-&gt;&gt;</code>.\n\nTo filter fast on a JSON key, I create a generated column from that key, then an index on that column.","syntaxe":"SELECT\n  data ->> '$.name',\n  JSON_EXTRACT(data,\n    '$.address.city')\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-61":{"q":"How do you query JSON in SQL Server?","a":"SQL Server offers several JSON functions. <code>JSON_VALUE()</code> reads a simple value, like a text or a number. <code>JSON_QUERY()</code> gets a whole object or list.\n\n<code>OPENJSON()</code> turns JSON into rows and columns, which is handy for analyzing a list of items.\n\nValues are referred to by a path, like <code>$.key</code>, and <code>ISJSON()</code> checks that a text is valid JSON.","syntaxe":"SELECT\n  JSON_VALUE(data,\n    '$.name'),\n  JSON_QUERY(data,\n    '$.address')\nFROM t\nWHERE ISJSON(data) = 1;"},"ent-v2-62":{"q":"What is an execution plan for?","a":"An execution plan shows how the database will run a query: which tables it reads, which indexes it uses, in what order it does the joins and sorts.\n\nThe execution plan helps understand why a query is slow. <code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the planned execution; <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> runs the query and adds the actual times and row counts.\n\nI mostly look for full scans of large tables and big gaps between the estimated and the actual rows.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, MySQL\nEXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT * FROM t\nWHERE a = 1;\n\n-- SQLite\nEXPLAIN QUERY PLAN\nSELECT * FROM t\nWHERE a = 1;"},"ent-v2-63":{"q":"Why might an index not be used?","a":"The database decides on its own how to run a query. It can ignore an index if it thinks another path costs less.\n\nCommon cases: the query returns a large part of the table, a function is applied to the column (like converting to uppercase), or a <code>LIKE</code> starts with <code>%</code>. Other cases: an index on several columns doesn’t start with the filtered column, or the table is too small for the index to help.\n\nTo check, I look at the execution plan with <code>EXPLAIN</code>: it shows the path the database chose.","syntaxe":"-- the index on a is ignored\nWHERE UPPER(a) = 'X'\nWHERE a LIKE '%x'\nWHERE a + 0 = 1\n\n-- it can be used\nWHERE a = 'X'\nWHERE a LIKE 'x%'"},"ent-v2-64":{"q":"What is index selectivity?","a":"Selectivity says how much a condition reduces the number of rows. The higher it is, the fewer rows are left to read.\n\nSearching by a unique identifier is very selective: only one row matches. Searching on a column that has only two or three possible values isn’t very selective: a large part of the table matches.\n\nAn index is mostly useful on a selective column. When a condition keeps a large part of the table, the database often prefers to read everything directly."},"ent-v2-65":{"q":"What are the four isolation levels defined by the SQL standard?","a":"The four levels are <code>READ UNCOMMITTED</code>, <code>READ COMMITTED</code>, <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> and <code>SERIALIZABLE</code>. These levels set what a transaction sees of the other transactions running at the same time.\n\nFrom the most flexible to the strictest: <code>READ UNCOMMITTED</code> can see changes not yet committed. <code>READ COMMITTED</code> sees only what’s committed. <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> guarantees that a row read again hasn’t changed. <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> acts as if the transactions ran one after another.\n\nThe stricter the level, the safer the data, but the more likely transactions are to wait or fail. <code>READ COMMITTED</code> is the most common default setting.","syntaxe":"SET TRANSACTION\n  ISOLATION LEVEL\n  READ COMMITTED;\n-- READ UNCOMMITTED,\n-- READ COMMITTED,\n-- REPEATABLE READ,\n-- SERIALIZABLE"},"ent-v2-66":{"q":"What is a deadlock?","a":"A deadlock happens when two transactions wait for each other: each one locks a row the other needs, and neither can move forward.\n\nTransaction A locks row 1, transaction B row 2; then A wants row 2, and B wants row 1. The database detects this dead end and cancels one of the two, so the other can finish.\n\nTo limit them, I always access rows in the same order, keep transactions short, and plan to retry the canceled transaction."},"ent-v2-67":{"q":"What is a dirty read?","a":"A dirty read means reading a change that another transaction hasn’t committed yet.\n\nIf that other transaction is then canceled, I’ve used data that never really existed.\n\nTo avoid it, I use the <code>READ COMMITTED</code> isolation level or stricter: it only shows committed changes. It’s the default setting in most databases."},"ent-v2-68":{"q":"What is MERGE for?","a":"<code>MERGE</code> updates a table from another one, in a single statement: rows that already exist are updated, and new ones are added.\n\nThe <code>ON</code> condition says how to recognize the same row in both tables. <code>WHEN MATCHED</code> describes what to do when it exists, <code>WHEN NOT MATCHED</code> when it doesn’t.\n\nI use it to sync data, for example during a regular load. MySQL and SQLite don’t have it: there I use an upsert, meaning an insert that turns into an update if the row already exists.","syntaxe":"MERGE INTO target c\nUSING source s\n  ON c.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN\n  UPDATE SET v = s.v\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN\n  INSERT (id, v)\n  VALUES (s.id, s.v);"},"ent-v2-69":{"q":"What’s the difference between a stored procedure and a stored function?","a":"A stored procedure is a routine saved in the database, often in several steps. I call it with <code>CALL</code> or <code>EXEC</code>, and it can change data.\n\nA stored function returns a value or a table of results. I use it directly in a query, like a built-in function.\n\nIn short: the procedure carries out a task, the function calculates a result. The exact rules vary by DBMS.","syntaxe":"-- procedure: CALL\nCALL my_procedure(1);\n\n-- function: inside a query\nSELECT my_function(a)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-70":{"q":"What is a trigger?","a":"A trigger is a routine the database runs on its own when a row is added, changed or deleted in a table.\n\nThe trigger can read the row before (<code>OLD</code>) and the row after (<code>NEW</code>). I use it to keep a history of changes, or to check a rule no constraint can express.\n\nThe downside: its action is invisible in the query that set it off, which makes debugging harder, and it slows down writes. So I use it sparingly.","syntaxe":"CREATE TRIGGER trg_upd\nAFTER UPDATE ON t\nFOR EACH ROW\nBEGIN\n  INSERT INTO log\n  VALUES (NEW.id);\nEND;"},"ent-v2-71":{"q":"What’s the difference between a view and a materialized view?","a":"A regular view is a saved query: its result is recalculated on every read.\n\nA materialized view saves the result itself. So reading it is much faster, especially for heavy calculations. In return, it isn’t updated automatically: it has to be refreshed.\n\nI choose a regular view when the data must always be up to date, and a materialized view when read speed matters more.","syntaxe":"CREATE VIEW v AS\n  SELECT …;\n\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW\n  mv AS SELECT …;\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW\n  mv;"},"ent-v2-72":{"q":"What is denormalization and why use it?","a":"Denormalizing means deliberately accepting to copy data, or to store already calculated results, so that some reads are faster.\n\nFor example, I store a total in the main table instead of recalculating it every time, or I copy a value to avoid a join.\n\nThe downside: these copies have to be kept up to date, otherwise the data contradicts itself. So I only do it for a real, measured performance need."},"ent-v2-73":{"q":"How do you protect against SQL injection?","a":"I use parameterized queries: the query contains placeholders (like <code>?</code>), and the user’s input is sent separately. The database always treats it as a plain value, never as SQL code.\n\nThe danger is pasting the input directly into the query text: a well-chosen input can then change the query, and for example show every row or delete some.\n\nOn top of that, I give the application’s account as few permissions as possible. Careful: a stored procedure that builds SQL from the input (<code>EXECUTE</code>) is still vulnerable.","syntaxe":"-- avoid\n\"… WHERE name = '\" + input\n  + \"'\"\n\n-- parameterized query\nSELECT *\nFROM t\nWHERE name = ?;"},"ent-v2-74":{"q":"How does pagination work with LIMIT/OFFSET or OFFSET/FETCH?","a":"Pagination shows results page by page, instead of returning everything at once.\n\n<code>LIMIT</code> sets the number of rows per page, and <code>OFFSET</code> the number of rows to skip: <code>LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20</code> gives the third page of 10. SQL Server and Oracle write the same thing with <code>OFFSET 20 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY</code>. I always add an <code>ORDER BY</code>, otherwise pages can get mixed up.\n\nOn large volumes, a big <code>OFFSET</code> gets slow, because the database still reads the skipped rows. So I start again from the last value shown, sorting on <code>id</code> with <code>WHERE id &gt; last_id</code>: that’s keyset pagination, much faster.","syntaxe":"SELECT * FROM t\nORDER BY id\nLIMIT 10 OFFSET 20;\n\n-- keyset\nSELECT * FROM t\nWHERE id > :last_id\nORDER BY id\nLIMIT 10;"},"ent-v2-75":{"q":"How do you find the second-highest salary in a table?","a":"I use <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> to rank the salaries from highest to lowest, then keep rank 2.\n\n<code>DENSE_RANK()</code> gives equal salaries the same rank, without skipping numbers: so rank 2 really is the second distinct salary, even if several people share the highest.\n\nWithout a window function, another approach also works: the largest salary among those below the maximum.","syntaxe":"WITH r AS (\n  SELECT salary,\n    DENSE_RANK() OVER (\n      ORDER BY salary DESC\n    ) AS n\n  FROM t\n)\nSELECT DISTINCT salary\nFROM r\nWHERE n = 2;"},"ent-v2-76":{"q":"What is SQL?","a":"SQL is the language used to talk to databases: read, add, change or delete data, and create tables.\n\nIt’s a language where I describe the result I want, not how to get it. The database finds the best path on its own.\n\nCommon follow-up: are SQL and MySQL the same thing? No: SQL is the language. MySQL, like PostgreSQL or SQL Server, is software that stores the data and understands SQL."},"ent-v2-77":{"q":"What is a relational database?","a":"A relational database stores data in tables, made of rows and columns, a bit like spreadsheets.\n\nThe tables are linked to each other by keys: a shared column, like a customer’s ID, also found in the orders table. Each piece of information is written only once. A join brings the tables together when I need it, thanks to that shared column.\n\nI query it in SQL, with software like PostgreSQL, MySQL or SQLite."},"ent-v2-78":{"q":"What’s the difference between a row and a column?","a":"A row describes one item: a customer, an order. A column describes the same piece of information for every item: the name, the date, the price.\n\nEach column has a type, like text, number or date. A cell holds a single value, or <code>NULL</code> if it’s empty.\n\nIn SQL, I choose the columns in the <code>SELECT</code> and the rows in the <code>WHERE</code>."},"ent-v2-79":{"q":"What’s the difference between DDL and DML?","a":"DDL, the data definition language, is used to create and change the structure: the tables and their columns, with <code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code> and <code>DROP</code>.\n\nDML, the data manipulation language, is used to work on the data itself: <code>INSERT</code> to add, <code>UPDATE</code> to change, <code>DELETE</code> to remove, and <code>SELECT</code> to read.\n\nTo remember it: DDL builds the tables, DML fills and changes them.","syntaxe":"-- DDL\nCREATE TABLE t (a INT);\nALTER TABLE t ADD b INT;\nDROP TABLE t;\n-- DML\nINSERT INTO t VALUES (1);\nUPDATE t SET a = 2;\nDELETE FROM t;"},"ent-v2-80":{"q":"What’s the difference between ALTER and UPDATE?","a":"<code>ALTER TABLE</code> changes a table’s structure, for example to add or drop a column. <code>UPDATE</code> changes the values in the rows, without touching the structure.\n\nThe two often go together: I add a column with <code>ALTER TABLE</code>, then fill it with <code>UPDATE</code>.\n\nCareful: an <code>UPDATE</code> without <code>WHERE</code> changes every row of the table. I always check my <code>WHERE</code> first.","syntaxe":"ALTER TABLE t\n  ADD COLUMN c TEXT;\n\nUPDATE t\nSET c = 'x'\nWHERE id = 1;"},"ent-v2-81":{"q":"What’s the difference between DISTINCT and UNIQUE?","a":"<code>DISTINCT</code> removes duplicates from a query’s result. <code>UNIQUE</code> is a rule set on the table, which prevents saving the same value twice.\n\n<code>DISTINCT</code> acts when I read the data, without changing anything in the table. <code>UNIQUE</code> acts when I write: a duplicate is rejected.","syntaxe":"-- when reading\nSELECT DISTINCT a FROM t;\n\n-- when writing\nALTER TABLE t\n  ADD UNIQUE (a);"},"ent-v2-82":{"q":"What is a SELF JOIN?","a":"A SELF JOIN means joining a table with itself. It isn’t a separate type of join: it’s a regular <code>JOIN</code> or <code>LEFT JOIN</code>, with two aliases to tell the table’s two roles apart.\n\nA SELF JOIN is useful when a row refers to another row of the same table: an employee and their manager, a category and its parent category.\n\nI use a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> to also keep the rows with no parent, at the top of the hierarchy. To go up several levels, I use a recursive CTE.","syntaxe":"SELECT e.name,\n  m.name AS parent\nFROM t AS e\nLEFT JOIN t AS m\n  ON m.id = e.parent_id;"},"ent-v2-83":{"q":"When should you use a join rather than a subquery?","a":"I use a join when I need to show columns from both tables. I use a subquery to filter, or to calculate an intermediate value, like a total.\n\nCareful: a join returns one row per match. So a row linked several times appears several times. A subquery with <code>IN</code> or <code>EXISTS</code> keeps each row only once.\n\nWhen both give the same result, I pick the more readable one: the database often runs them the same way.","syntaxe":"-- join: to show\nSELECT t1.a, t2.b\nFROM t1\nJOIN t2 ON t2.t1_id = t1.id;\n\n-- subquery: to filter\nSELECT * FROM t1\nWHERE id IN (\n  SELECT t1_id FROM t2);"},"ent-v2-84":{"q":"What’s the difference between a clustered and a non-clustered index?","a":"A clustered index decides the order in which the rows are stored in the table. So a table can have only one clustered index, often on the primary key.\n\nA non-clustered index is a separate list that points to the rows without changing how they’re stored. A table can have several.\n\nA simple picture: the clustered index is a binder whose sheets are filed by date; the non-clustered index is the index at the back of a book, which gives the pages without moving them.","syntaxe":"-- SQL Server\nCREATE CLUSTERED INDEX\n  ix_c ON t (id);\nCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX\n  ix_n ON t (a);"},"ent-v2-85":{"q":"How do you analyze a slow query?","a":"I start by measuring its run time, then I look at its execution plan with <code>EXPLAIN</code>: it shows how the database reads the tables.\n\nI mostly look for full scans of large tables, costly joins and big sorts. Then I check whether an index is missing, whether the filters are written well, and whether I’m selecting useless columns.\n\nAfter each change, I measure again, to check that the query really is faster.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, MySQL\nEXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT …;\n-- SQLite\nEXPLAIN QUERY PLAN\nSELECT …;"},"ent-v2-86":{"q":"What is LIKE for, and what do % and _ mean?","a":"<code>LIKE</code> looks for text that matches a pattern.\n\nIn that pattern, <code>%</code> stands for any sequence of characters, even none, and <code>_</code> stands for exactly one character.\n\n<code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> finds the texts that start with “abc”, <code>LIKE '%abc'</code> the ones that end with “abc”, and <code>LIKE '%abc%'</code> the ones that contain “abc”.","syntaxe":"WHERE name LIKE 'ab%'\nWHERE name LIKE '%ab'\nWHERE name LIKE 'a_c'\nWHERE name NOT LIKE '%ab%'\nWHERE c LIKE '%!%%'\n  ESCAPE '!'"},"ent-v2-87":{"q":"What’s the difference between RIGHT JOIN and FULL OUTER JOIN?","a":"<code>RIGHT JOIN</code> keeps every row of the right table, with empty cells when nothing matches on the left. It’s the mirror of <code>LEFT JOIN</code>.\n\n<code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> keeps every row of both tables: the ones that match, and also the ones that exist on only one side. A <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> is used to compare two data sources.\n\nIn practice, I often rewrite a <code>RIGHT JOIN</code> as a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>, swapping the tables: it’s easier to read. MySQL has no <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code>: there I combine the results of a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> and a <code>RIGHT JOIN</code> with <code>UNION</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT *\nFROM t1\nFULL OUTER JOIN t2\n  ON t2.t1_id = t1.id;"},"ent-v2-88":{"q":"What is a CROSS JOIN for?","a":"<code>CROSS JOIN</code> pairs each row of one table with each row of the other, with no condition: every possible combination.\n\nI use it to generate every combination, for example each product for each month. A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> on the real data then fills in this grid. A combination with no data still shows up, with an empty cell (<code>NULL</code>) I can replace with 0, instead of disappearing.\n\nWatch the size: two tables of 1,000 rows give a million rows.","syntaxe":"SELECT a.x, b.y\nFROM a\nCROSS JOIN b;"},"ent-v2-89":{"q":"How do you gather a group’s values into a single cell?","a":"I use a function that joins each group’s values into a single text, with a separator: <code>GROUP_CONCAT</code> in SQLite and MySQL, <code>STRING_AGG</code> in PostgreSQL and SQL Server, <code>LISTAGG</code> in Oracle.\n\nWith a <code>GROUP BY</code>, I get one row per group, with all its values gathered in a single cell, separated by a comma or the separator of my choice.\n\nWithout a specified sort, the order of the values isn’t guaranteed: I add an <code>ORDER BY</code> inside the function. Empty cells (<code>NULL</code>) are ignored.","syntaxe":"-- SQLite, MySQL\nGROUP_CONCAT(v, ', ')\n-- PostgreSQL, SQL Server\nSTRING_AGG(v, ', ')\n-- Oracle\nLISTAGG(v, ', ')\n  WITHIN GROUP\n  (ORDER BY v)"},"ent-v2-90":{"q":"What are INTERSECT and EXCEPT for?","a":"<code>INTERSECT</code> keeps the rows found in both results. <code>EXCEPT</code> keeps the rows of the first result that aren’t in the second (Oracle calls it <code>MINUS</code>).\n\nAs with <code>UNION</code>, both queries must have the same number of columns, and duplicates are removed.\n\nWith <code>EXCEPT</code>, the order of the queries matters: A except B isn’t B except A. An advantage over <code>NOT IN</code>: <code>NULL</code> values cause no trouble.","syntaxe":"SELECT a FROM t1\nINTERSECT\nSELECT a FROM t2;\n\nSELECT a FROM t1\nEXCEPT\nSELECT a FROM t2;"},"ent-v2-91":{"q":"How do you convert a type with CAST?","a":"<code>CAST(value AS type)</code> converts a value to another type: a text to a number or a date, a number to a text.\n\nTypical case: numbers stored as text sort character by character, and <code>'100'</code> comes before <code>'9'</code>. By sorting on <code>CAST(col AS INTEGER)</code>, I get the numeric order back.\n\nThe real fix is often to give the column the right type: <code>CAST</code> corrects on every read, and on a filtered column, it usually prevents its index from being used.","syntaxe":"SELECT CAST(a AS INTEGER)\nFROM t\nORDER BY CAST(a AS INTEGER);"},"ent-v2-92":{"q":"Which text functions do you use the most?","a":"I mostly use <code>UPPER</code> and <code>LOWER</code> to convert to uppercase or lowercase, and <code>TRIM</code> to remove extra spaces. I also use <code>SUBSTR</code> to extract a piece of text, <code>REPLACE</code> to replace a word, and <code>LENGTH</code> to count characters.\n\nThese functions are mostly used to clean data: to the database, “Paris” and “paris” aren’t the same value.\n\nThe syntax changes a little from one database system, called a DBMS, to another. These variants are gathered in “See by DBMS”.","syntaxe":"SELECT\n  UPPER(a), LOWER(a),\n  TRIM(a),\n  SUBSTR(a, 1, 3),\n  REPLACE(a, 'x', 'y'),\n  LENGTH(a)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-93":{"q":"How do you calculate the number of days between two dates?","a":"I use a date function, which changes with the DBMS: <code>DATEDIFF</code> in MySQL and SQL Server, a simple subtraction in PostgreSQL, <code>JULIANDAY()</code> in SQLite.\n\nThe order of the dates in the function isn’t the same everywhere: I always check the sign of the result.\n\nWatch out for dates that include a time: the result can count fractions of a day. Each DBMS’s syntax is gathered in “See by DBMS”.","syntaxe":"-- MySQL\nDATEDIFF(end_date, start_date)\n-- SQL Server\nDATEDIFF(day, start_date, end_date)\n-- PostgreSQL\nend_date - start_date\n-- SQLite\nJULIANDAY(end_date)\n  - JULIANDAY(start_date)"},"ent-v2-94":{"q":"What does ON DELETE CASCADE do?","a":"<code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> is set on a foreign key: when I delete a parent row, the rows that refer to it are deleted along with it.\n\nWithout this option, the database refuses to delete a row still used elsewhere. Another possible choice: <code>SET NULL</code>, which keeps the linked rows but empties their reference.\n\nI keep the cascade for data that makes no sense without its parent. I avoid it for what must be kept, like a history or invoices.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE child (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  parent_id INTEGER\n    REFERENCES parent (id)\n    ON DELETE CASCADE\n);"},"ent-v2-95":{"q":"What are CHECK and DEFAULT for?","a":"<code>CHECK</code> checks that a value follows a rule, for example that a quantity is positive. If the rule isn’t met, the row is rejected.\n\n<code>DEFAULT</code> gives an automatic value when I don’t provide one, for example zero or today’s date.\n\nI add them when creating the table, on the relevant columns.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE t (\n  qty INTEGER\n    CHECK (qty > 0),\n  created_on DATE\n    DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE\n);"},"ent-v2-96":{"q":"How do you insert a row, or update it if it already exists?","a":"That’s an “upsert”: add the row, or update it if it already exists. I write <code>ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE</code> in PostgreSQL and SQLite, <code>ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE</code> in MySQL, and <code>MERGE</code> in SQL Server.\n\nWith <code>ON CONFLICT</code> and <code>ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE</code>, the database spots an existing row through a <code>UNIQUE</code> column or the primary key. With <code>ON CONFLICT</code>, the word <code>excluded</code> refers to the row I wanted to add: I reuse its values for the update.\n\nIt’s safer than checking first with a <code>SELECT</code>, then inserting: in between, someone else may add the same row. To simply ignore duplicates, I write <code>ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code>, or <code>INSERT IGNORE</code> in MySQL.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, SQLite\nINSERT INTO t (id, v)\nVALUES (1, 'x')\nON CONFLICT (id)\nDO UPDATE\n  SET v = excluded.v;\n\n-- MySQL\nINSERT INTO t (id, v)\nVALUES (1, 'x')\nON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE\n  v = VALUES(v);"},"ent-v2-97":{"q":"How do you grant and revoke permissions?","a":"<code>GRANT</code> gives a permission (read, insert, update or delete) on a table to a user or a role. <code>REVOKE</code> takes it away.\n\nEach permission is separate: I can allow reading without allowing changes.\n\nI give as few permissions as possible, and I give them to roles rather than to people: it’s easier to manage when someone changes jobs.","syntaxe":"GRANT SELECT, INSERT\n  ON t TO role_x;\n\nREVOKE INSERT\n  ON t FROM role_x;"},"ent-v2-98":{"q":"What are schemas in a database for?","a":"A schema is a folder inside a database: it groups tables, views and functions.\n\nA schema is used to organize objects by topic, avoid name conflicts (two tables can have the same name in two different schemas) and manage permissions by topic. I then refer to a table as <code>schema.table</code>.\n\nNot to be confused with the star schema, which is a way of organizing tables for analysis.","syntaxe":"CREATE SCHEMA sales;\n\nCREATE TABLE sales.t (\n  id INTEGER\n);\n\nSELECT * FROM sales.t;"},"ent-v2-99":{"q":"How do you calculate month-over-month revenue growth in SQL?","a":"First I calculate each month’s revenue with a <code>GROUP BY</code>. Then <code>LAG()</code> puts the previous month’s figure next to each month. <code>LAG()</code> is a window function, which reads another row without grouping the rows: I write <code>LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month)</code> to follow the order of the months.\n\nThe growth as a percentage is the difference between the two months, divided by the previous month, times 100.\n\nTo avoid dividing by zero, I write <code>NULLIF(prev_month, 0)</code>: if the previous month is 0, <code>NULLIF</code> replaces it with <code>NULL</code>, and the calculation gives <code>NULL</code> instead of an error. The first month has no previous month: its growth is <code>NULL</code>. I also check that no month is missing, otherwise the comparison is made with an older month.","syntaxe":"WITH m AS (\n  SELECT month,\n    SUM(v) AS revenue\n  FROM t\n  GROUP BY month\n)\nSELECT month, revenue,\n  100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue)\n    OVER (ORDER BY month))\n  / NULLIF(LAG(revenue) OVER\n    (ORDER BY month), 0)\n  AS growth_pct\nFROM m;"},"ent-v2-100":{"q":"How do you calculate a median in SQL?","a":"The median is the middle value once the values are sorted. Unlike the average, it’s barely affected by extreme values.\n\nWhen the DBMS allows it (PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server), I use <code>PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY col)</code>. The <code>0.5</code> means 50%: half the values are below, the other half above. SQL Server also requires an <code>OVER ()</code>.\n\nOtherwise, I number the sorted values with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, count them, and keep the middle one. If there’s an even number of values, I average the two in the middle.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, Oracle\nSELECT PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5)\n  WITHIN GROUP\n  (ORDER BY v)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-101":{"q":"What’s the difference between OLTP and OLAP?","a":"OLTP, for “online transaction processing”, runs day-to-day operations: recording a sale, changing a booking. These are many small, fast operations, on a few rows at a time.\n\nOLAP, for “online analytical processing”, is for analysis: calculating totals, comparing periods, feeding dashboards. These are fewer queries, but over many rows, often in a data warehouse: a database set aside for analysis, separate from the application’s.\n\nIn short: OLTP records the operations, OLAP analyzes them."},"ent-v2-102":{"q":"What is a star schema?","a":"A star schema organizes data for analysis. In the center, a fact table holds what I measure: amounts, quantities. Around it, dimension tables describe the context: who, what, when, where.\n\nThe dimension tables are linked to the fact table by keys, which draws a star.\n\nI filter and group by the dimensions, then calculate on the facts. Queries stay simple, and dashboard tools understand it well."},"ent-v2-103":{"q":"How do you sort NULLs first or last?","a":"I add <code>NULLS FIRST</code> or <code>NULLS LAST</code> after the sort column, to put the empty cells at the start or the end.\n\nIf you don’t specify, it depends on the DBMS: some put <code>NULL</code> values first, others last. And this syntax doesn’t exist everywhere: in MySQL and SQL Server, I first sort on “is the value empty?”, then on the column.","syntaxe":"SELECT *\nFROM t\nORDER BY a NULLS LAST;"},"ent-v2-104":{"q":"Can you sort by an alias or by a column number?","a":"Yes to both. I can sort by an alias, because the sort comes after the <code>SELECT</code>. And <code>ORDER BY 2</code> sorts by the second column of the <code>SELECT</code>.\n\nI prefer the alias: I can see right away what the sort is based on. With a number, if I change the order of the columns, the sort changes too, without warning.","syntaxe":"SELECT a,\n  SUM(b) AS total\nFROM t\nGROUP BY a\nORDER BY total DESC;\n-- or ORDER BY 2 DESC"},"ent-v2-105":{"q":"What’s the difference between COUNT(*) and COUNT(1)?","a":"No difference: both count every row, and they’re just as fast as each other.\n\nI prefer <code>COUNT(*)</code>, the syntax designed for counting rows.\n\nThe real difference is with <code>COUNT(col)</code>, which counts only the rows where the column is filled in.","syntaxe":"SELECT COUNT(*),\n  COUNT(1),\n  COUNT(col)\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-106":{"q":"What is a 1:1 relationship, and when do you use it?","a":"In a 1:1 (one-to-one) relationship, a row of one table matches a single row of the other table, and vice versa.\n\nI create it with a foreign key that can’t repeat (<code>UNIQUE</code>): each row of the first table can have only one linked row.\n\nI use it to set aside optional or sensitive information, in a separate table.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE detail (\n  t_id INTEGER UNIQUE\n    REFERENCES t (id),\n  info TEXT\n);"},"ent-v2-107":{"q":"What’s the difference between a temporary table and a CTE?","a":"A CTE, written with <code>WITH</code>, is an intermediate result that exists only during a single query. A temporary table is a real table, which stays available until the end of the session.\n\nI use a CTE to make a query more readable, by breaking it into steps. I use a temporary table when several queries need the same result: I calculate it only once.","syntaxe":"WITH x AS (SELECT …)\nSELECT * FROM x;\n\nCREATE TEMPORARY TABLE x AS\n  SELECT …;\nSELECT * FROM x;"},"ent-v2-108":{"q":"How do you group dates by month or by week?","a":"I use a function that brings each date back to the start of its month or week, then a <code>GROUP BY</code> on that result.\n\nI can then count, add up or average for each month or each week.\n\nOnly the function changes with the DBMS, for example <code>DATE_TRUNC</code> in PostgreSQL: the syntaxes are in “See by DBMS”.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('month',\n  d) AS month,\n  COUNT(*)\nFROM t\nGROUP BY 1;"},"ent-v2-109":{"q":"What’s the difference between SQL and NoSQL?","a":"A SQL database stores data in well-defined tables, linked to each other, and it guarantees that the data stays consistent.\n\n“NoSQL” covers other ways of storing data: documents (like MongoDB), key-value pairs (like Redis) or graphs. Their structure is often more flexible.\n\nI choose based on the need: SQL when the data is well structured and interlinked, NoSQL when it often changes shape or must be very fast to read."},"ent-v2-110":{"q":"What’s the difference between NULL, zero and an empty string?","a":"Zero and the empty text <code>''</code> are real values: a number equal to 0, a text with no characters. <code>NULL</code> means there’s no value at all: it’s missing or unknown.\n\nThe difference shows in calculations: a number plus zero stays the same number, but a number plus <code>NULL</code> gives <code>NULL</code>. And an average counts zeros, but ignores <code>NULL</code> values.","syntaxe":"SELECT COUNT(a), AVG(a)\nFROM t;\n-- ignore NULLs,\n-- count 0 and ''"},"ent-v2-111":{"q":"Why do <> and != exclude rows containing NULL?","a":"<code>NULL</code> isn’t a value, it’s an unknown value. Comparing an unknown value with “x” gives neither true nor false, but “unknown”.\n\nBut <code>WHERE</code> keeps only the rows where the condition is true: so the rows with <code>NULL</code> disappear, with <code>=</code> as well as with <code>&lt;&gt;</code>.\n\nTo keep them, I write it out clearly: “different from x, or empty”, with <code>OR col IS NULL</code>.","syntaxe":"WHERE a <> 'x'\n  OR a IS NULL"},"ent-v2-112":{"q":"What is a SQL dialect?","a":"A SQL dialect is the version of the language specific to one piece of software: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server and SQLite all speak SQL, but each with its own quirks.\n\nThe basics are the same everywhere: <code>SELECT</code>, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>JOIN</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>. The differences are mostly in some functions, like dates or limiting the number of rows.\n\nIn an interview, I say which dialect I use.\n\nTwo names come up often: T-SQL and PL/SQL. T-SQL is SQL Server’s dialect; PL/SQL is Oracle’s language for writing programs around SQL. PostgreSQL has the equivalent with PL/pgSQL."},"ent-v2-113":{"q":"Are LEFT JOIN and LEFT OUTER JOIN the same thing?","a":"Yes: the word <code>OUTER</code> is optional. <code>LEFT JOIN</code> and <code>LEFT OUTER JOIN</code> give exactly the same result.\n\nIn the same way, <code>JOIN</code> on its own means <code>INNER JOIN</code>.","syntaxe":"FROM t1\nLEFT JOIN t2 ON …\n-- same as\nFROM t1\nLEFT OUTER JOIN t2 ON …"},"ent-v2-114":{"q":"How do you pick random rows?","a":"I sort the rows randomly, then keep the first ones: <code>ORDER BY RANDOM()</code> followed by a limit. The function’s name changes with the DBMS, like <code>RAND()</code> in MySQL.\n\nEach run gives a different draw.\n\nOn a very large table, it’s slow, because the database has to shuffle everything before choosing.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, SQLite\nSELECT * FROM t\nORDER BY RANDOM()\nLIMIT 3;\n-- MySQL: RAND()\n-- SQL Server:\nSELECT TOP 3 * FROM t\nORDER BY NEWID();"},"ent-v2-115":{"q":"How do you insert several rows in a single query?","a":"I write a single <code>INSERT INTO</code>, then several groups of values after <code>VALUES</code>, separated by commas. Each group in parentheses becomes a row.\n\nIt’s faster than sending one query per row. And if one row causes a problem, usually none of them is added.","syntaxe":"INSERT INTO t (a, b)\nVALUES\n  (1, 'x'),\n  (2, 'y'),\n  (3, 'z');"},"ent-v2-116":{"q":"In a LEFT JOIN, what’s the difference between filtering in ON and in WHERE?","a":"In a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>, a condition placed in <code>ON</code> only chooses which right rows to pair: every left row stays. Placed in <code>WHERE</code>, it filters after the join, and can make left rows disappear.\n\nWhy? Left rows with no match have empty cells on the right. A condition on the right table in the <code>WHERE</code> isn’t true on those empty cells: those rows are removed, and the <code>LEFT JOIN</code> behaves like an <code>INNER JOIN</code>.\n\nA simple rule: a filter on the right table goes in <code>ON</code>, a filter on the left table goes in <code>WHERE</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT *\nFROM t1\nLEFT JOIN t2\n  ON t2.t1_id = t1.id\n  AND t2.v > 10 -- right\nWHERE t1.ok = 1; -- left"},"ent-v2-117":{"q":"What is a non-equi join?","a":"It’s a join whose condition isn’t an equality, but a comparison: greater than, less than, or between two values.\n\nThis join is mostly used to place a value in a bracket: an amount in a price grid, a date in a period.\n\nFor brackets that follow each other without overlapping, I write “greater than or equal to the start” and “strictly less than the end”. With <code>BETWEEN</code>, both bounds are included, and a boundary value would fall into two brackets.","syntaxe":"SELECT t.v, g.bracket\nFROM t\nJOIN grid AS g\n  ON t.v >= g.min\n  AND t.v < g.max;"},"ent-v2-118":{"q":"What happens when a subquery after = returns several rows?","a":"After an <code>=</code>, the subquery must return a single value. If it returns several, most databases stop the query with an error; SQLite, for its part, takes the first one without warning.\n\nThe trap: the query can work for a long time, then fail the day a second row shows up in the data.\n\nIf several values are possible, I use <code>IN</code>. If I want a single value, I guarantee it with <code>MAX()</code> or a limit. And I ask myself why there are several rows: a duplicate, a forgotten condition?","syntaxe":"-- a single value\nWHERE a = (\n  SELECT MAX(b) FROM t2)\n\n-- several values\nWHERE a IN (\n  SELECT b FROM t2)"},"ent-v2-119":{"q":"How do you calculate an average order value?","a":"The average order value is the total revenue divided by the number of purchases.\n\nIf each row already holds the amount of one purchase, a simple average is enough. If the amounts are spread over detail lines, I first calculate the total of each purchase, then the average of those totals: otherwise I’d get the average amount of a line, not of a purchase.\n\nI also specify what I count: only paid purchases, with or without taxes, with or without discounts.","syntaxe":"WITH purchases AS (\n  SELECT purchase_id,\n    SUM(price * qty) AS total\n  FROM lines\n  GROUP BY purchase_id\n)\nSELECT AVG(total)\nFROM purchases;"},"ent-v2-120":{"q":"How do you find each customer’s first purchase?","a":"I number each customer’s purchases, from oldest to newest, with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, then keep purchase number 1 for each one.\n\n<code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> is a window function: each row gets a number, and no row is grouped. I write <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY purchase_date)</code>: <code>PARTITION BY</code> restarts the numbering at 1 for each customer, and <code>ORDER BY</code> follows the order of the dates.\n\nThe <code>WHERE</code> is read before the numbers are calculated: so the filter can’t go there directly. I calculate the number in a CTE, then filter on it. That way I get the whole purchase row, not just its date. If only the date matters, a <code>MIN()</code> with <code>GROUP BY</code> on the customer is enough.","syntaxe":"WITH r AS (\n  SELECT *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY customer_id\n      ORDER BY d, id) AS n\n  FROM purchases\n)\nSELECT *\nFROM r\nWHERE n = 1;"},"ent-v2-121":{"q":"Natural key or surrogate key: which one should you choose?","a":"A natural key comes from the data itself: an email, a license plate number. A surrogate key is a plain number with no meaning, assigned by the database.\n\nMost of the time I choose a surrogate key as the primary key: it never changes and stays short. If an email changes, no other table needs updating.\n\nI still keep the natural key as <code>UNIQUE NOT NULL</code>, to avoid duplicates. The automatic number is written differently depending on the DBMS, for example <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> in MySQL or <code>GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY</code> in PostgreSQL.","syntaxe":"CREATE TABLE t (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  email TEXT\n    UNIQUE NOT NULL\n);"},"ent-v2-122":{"q":"What is NTILE() and what is it for?","a":"<code>NTILE(n)</code> splits the sorted rows into n roughly equal groups, and gives each row the number of its group.\n\n<code>NTILE(4)</code> splits into quarters (quartiles): with a descending sort, group 1 holds the quarter of the highest values. <code>NTILE(10)</code> splits into tenths. I use it to segment a population, for example the best customers.\n\nCareful: the split is based on position, not value. So two equal rows can land in two different groups.","syntaxe":"SELECT a, v,\n  NTILE(4) OVER (\n    ORDER BY v DESC\n  ) AS quartile\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-123":{"q":"How do you find employees paid more than their department’s average?","a":"I compare each employee’s salary with the average of their own department.\n\nThe simplest way: a window function, <code>AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department)</code>, which adds the department’s average next to each row. I calculate it in a CTE, then keep the rows where the salary is higher than that average.\n\nAnother way: a correlated subquery, which recalculates the department’s average for each employee. The result is the same, but it’s often slower.","syntaxe":"WITH m AS (\n  SELECT *,\n    AVG(salary) OVER (\n      PARTITION BY department\n    ) AS avg_salary\n  FROM t\n)\nSELECT *\nFROM m\nWHERE salary > avg_salary;"},"ent-v2-124":{"q":"How do you find the customers who ordered in both January and February?","a":"I group the orders by customer, keeping only the ones from January and February, then keep the customers who do have both months.\n\nConcretely: <code>GROUP BY</code> on the customer, then <code>HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT month) = 2</code>. The <code>DISTINCT</code> matters: without it, two orders in February would count as 2.\n\nAnother method: the intersection (<code>INTERSECT</code>) of January’s customers and February’s. Either way, I filter on precise date ranges, so as not to mix up the years.","syntaxe":"SELECT customer_id\nFROM orders\nWHERE d >= '2026-01-01'\n  AND d < '2026-03-01'\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT\n  STRFTIME('%m', d)) = 2;"},"ent-v2-125":{"q":"How do you keep only the most recent row for each customer?","a":"I number each customer’s rows, from newest to oldest, with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, then keep only row number 1.\n\n<code>PARTITION BY</code> restarts the numbering for each customer, and sorting by descending date gives number 1 to the newest. If two rows have the same date, I add the ID to the sort to break the tie: otherwise, the row kept can change from one run to the next.\n\nThe <code>WHERE</code> is read before the numbers are calculated: so I can’t filter on the number in the same query. I calculate the number in a CTE or a subquery, then filter in the query that uses it.","syntaxe":"WITH r AS (\n  SELECT *,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY customer_id\n      ORDER BY d DESC, id DESC\n    ) AS n\n  FROM t\n)\nSELECT *\nFROM r\nWHERE n = 1;"},"ent-v2-126":{"q":"How do you update a table from another one?","a":"I link the table to update to the one holding the new values, then copy those values over. The syntax depends on the DBMS: <code>UPDATE … FROM</code> in PostgreSQL, SQLite and SQL Server, <code>UPDATE … JOIN</code> in MySQL.\n\nOnly the rows that find a match are updated: the others keep their value.\n\nTo also add the missing rows in the same statement, I use <code>MERGE</code> or an upsert. An upsert, a blend of “update” and “insert”, adds the row if it doesn’t exist yet, and updates it otherwise. <code>MERGE</code> does the same by comparing a whole source table with the table to update.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, SQLite,\n-- SQL Server\nUPDATE t\nSET v = s.v\nFROM s\nWHERE s.id = t.id;\n\n-- MySQL\nUPDATE t\nJOIN s ON s.id = t.id\nSET t.v = s.v;"},"ent-v2-127":{"q":"Can you create a view from another view, and what happens to it if the table is dropped?","a":"Yes: a view can be built on another view, just as on a table.\n\nBut a view only holds its query, not data. If the original table is dropped, the views that depend on it stop working. Depending on the DBMS, the drop is refused (PostgreSQL), or accepted, and the views then fail when read.\n\nStacking views is allowed, but over time it becomes hard to know what a query really does.","syntaxe":"CREATE VIEW v1 AS\n  SELECT … FROM t;\nCREATE VIEW v2 AS\n  SELECT … FROM v1;"},"ent-v2-128":{"q":"How do you keep rows from multiplying when joining three tables?","a":"The problem: I join customers to their orders and to their payments. Each of a customer’s orders is then repeated for each payment of that same customer. Totals and counts become wrong.\n\nWith 2 orders and 3 payments for one customer, the join produces 2 × 3 = 6 rows, and a sum counts each amount several times.\n\nThe fix: first calculate each total separately, in CTEs, so there’s only one row per customer, then join those results. To spot the problem, I compare the number of rows before and after the join.","syntaxe":"WITH a AS (\n  SELECT t_id, SUM(x) AS sx\n  FROM t1 GROUP BY t_id),\nb AS (\n  SELECT t_id, COUNT(*) AS n\n  FROM t2 GROUP BY t_id)\nSELECT t.id, a.sx, b.n\nFROM t\nLEFT JOIN a ON a.t_id = t.id\nLEFT JOIN b ON b.t_id = t.id;"},"ent-v2-129":{"q":"What are ROLLUP, CUBE and GROUPING SETS for?","a":"They’re <code>GROUP BY</code> options that calculate several levels of totals in a single query: the detail, the subtotals and the grand total.\n\n<code>ROLLUP</code> follows a hierarchy: by year and month, then by year, then the total. <code>CUBE</code> calculates every possible combination. <code>GROUPING SETS</code> lists exactly the groupings you want.\n\nOn a subtotal row, the summarized column is empty (<code>NULL</code>). The <code>GROUPING()</code> function lets you recognize it, for example to show “Total” instead. MySQL only has <code>ROLLUP</code>, and SQLite has none of the three.","syntaxe":"SELECT a, b, SUM(v)\nFROM t\nGROUP BY ROLLUP (a, b);\n\nGROUP BY CUBE (a, b)\nGROUP BY GROUPING SETS\n  ((a, b), (a), ())"},"ent-v2-130":{"q":"What is LATERAL (or CROSS APPLY)?","a":"<code>LATERAL</code> lets a subquery placed in the <code>FROM</code> use the columns of the tables written before it. The subquery is then recalculated for each row, a bit like a loop. SQL Server writes <code>CROSS APPLY</code>.\n\nIt’s handy for getting “the last 3 orders of each customer”: for each customer, the subquery keeps their orders, sorts them from newest to oldest, then stops at 3.\n\nWithout <code>LATERAL</code>, I get the same result with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> and <code>PARTITION BY</code>: I number each customer’s orders, then keep numbers 1 to 3. SQLite doesn’t support <code>LATERAL</code>.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL, MySQL\nSELECT t1.id, d.*\nFROM t1\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n  SELECT *\n  FROM t2\n  WHERE t2.t1_id = t1.id\n  ORDER BY t2.d DESC\n  LIMIT 3\n) AS d;"},"ent-v2-131":{"q":"How do you calculate a retention rate by cohort?","a":"A cohort is a group of users who started in the same period, for example the same month. Retention is the share of that group still active in the following months.\n\nI put the users into cohorts based on their first activity, then follow each cohort over the periods: for each month, I count the active users, and divide by the cohort’s starting size.\n\nTo compare cohorts with each other, I line them up by age (month 0, month 1, month 2…) rather than by calendar.","syntaxe":"SELECT u, month,\n  MIN(month) OVER (\n    PARTITION BY u\n  ) AS cohort\nFROM activity;"},"ent-v2-132":{"q":"How do you solve a “gaps and islands” problem?","a":"A “gaps and islands” problem is about spotting continuous runs, the islands, and the gaps between them: for example consecutive login days.\n\nThe trick: I number each user’s days with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, then subtract that number from the date. As long as the days follow each other, the date and the number move forward together, and the result stays the same. Example: the 3rd, 4th and 5th of the month get numbers 1, 2 and 3, and all give the 2nd of the month. That result acts as the run’s label.\n\nA <code>GROUP BY</code> on this label gives the start, the end and the length of each run. I first remove duplicate days, otherwise the numbering gets shifted.","syntaxe":"SELECT u,\n  MIN(day) AS start_day,\n  MAX(day) AS end_day\nFROM (\n  SELECT u, day,\n    day - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY u\n      ORDER BY day) AS grp\n  FROM t\n) AS x\nGROUP BY u, grp;"},"ent-v2-133":{"q":"Optimistic or pessimistic locking?","a":"A lock prevents other users from changing a row while I’m working on it. A pessimistic lock blocks the row as soon as I read it, with <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> in a transaction: the others wait for my transaction to end. It’s safe, but it creates waiting.\n\nAn optimistic lock blocks nothing. Each row carries a version number. When saving, I update the row only if that number hasn’t changed since I read it, with <code>WHERE version = …</code> in the <code>UPDATE</code>, and I increase the number. If no row is updated, someone got there before me, and the application handles the conflict.\n\nI choose pessimistic when conflicts are frequent, optimistic when they’re rare.","syntaxe":"-- pessimistic\nSELECT * FROM t\nWHERE id = 1\nFOR UPDATE;\n\n-- optimistic\nUPDATE t\nSET v = 'x',\n  version = version + 1\nWHERE id = 1\n  AND version = 12;"},"ent-v2-134":{"q":"What is table partitioning?","a":"Partitioning a table means splitting it into pieces based on a column, often a date: one piece per month, for example. For my queries, it’s still a single table.\n\nThe advantage: a query filtered on one month reads only that month’s piece. And deleting an old month is very fast: I remove its whole piece instead of deleting the rows one by one.\n\nWithout a filter on the partitioning column, every piece is read. So partitioning doesn’t replace indexes, which quickly find a few specific rows, like the index at the back of a book. Partitioning is only useful on very large tables.","syntaxe":"-- PostgreSQL\nCREATE TABLE t (\n  d DATE, v INT\n) PARTITION BY RANGE (d);\n\nCREATE TABLE t_2026_01\n  PARTITION OF t\n  FOR VALUES\n  FROM ('2026-01-01')\n  TO ('2026-02-01');"},"ent-v2-135":{"q":"What is a slowly changing dimension (SCD type 2)?","a":"A type 2 slowly changing dimension keeps the history of changes. A dimension is a table that describes the context, for example customers. When a piece of information changes, I don’t replace the row: I close it with an end date, and add a new row with the new value.\n\nEach version has its own surrogate key, a plain number assigned by the database. The facts, meaning measures like sales, point to that key: each sale stays linked to the version valid when it happened. The current version has no end date.\n\nFor comparison: type 1 simply overwrites the old value, with no history; type 3 keeps only the previous value in another column.","syntaxe":"SELECT *\nFROM dim\nWHERE business_id = 42\n  AND start_date <= :d\n  AND (end_date IS NULL\n    OR end_date >= :d);"},"ent-v2-136":{"q":"How do you calculate a 7-day moving average?","a":"I use <code>AVG()</code> as a window function: for each day, the average covers that day’s row and the 6 previous rows. I write <code>AVG(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)</code>. That last part is called the frame: the list of rows taken into the calculation.\n\nCareful: <code>ROWS</code> counts rows, not days. If days are missing from the table, the 7 rows cover more than a week. So I first fill in the calendar, with a date table and zeros for empty days.\n\nSome DBMSs, like PostgreSQL, also accept a <code>RANGE</code> frame over a duration, which directly takes “the previous 6 days”.","syntaxe":"SELECT d, v,\n  AVG(v) OVER (\n    ORDER BY d\n    ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING\n    AND CURRENT ROW\n  ) AS avg_7d\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-137":{"q":"How do you calculate a cumulative percentage of revenue?","a":"I sort the items from highest to lowest revenue, then calculate a running total with <code>SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY revenue DESC)</code>: each row gets the sum of its value and all the ones before it. I divide this running total by the grand total, which <code>SUM(revenue) OVER ()</code> puts on every row.\n\nEach row then shows the share of revenue made by that item and the ones before it. It’s the basis of the 80/20 rule (Pareto analysis): what small share of the items makes most of the total?\n\nIn PostgreSQL, SQLite or SQL Server, a division between two whole numbers keeps only the whole part: <code>1 / 4</code> gives <code>0</code>. So I start by multiplying by <code>100.0</code>, not <code>100</code>, to get a decimal result.","syntaxe":"SELECT a, revenue,\n  100.0 * SUM(revenue) OVER (\n    ORDER BY revenue DESC)\n  / SUM(revenue) OVER ()\n  AS cumulative_pct\nFROM t;"},"ent-v2-138":{"q":"How do you use SQL to measure the conversion rate between two steps of a user journey?","a":"I count the distinct users who reached each step, then divide the number for the second step by the number for the first.\n\n<code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN step = '…' THEN user_id END)</code> does this count. The <code>CASE WHEN</code> returns the ID for the rows of the targeted step, and <code>NULL</code> for the others, which <code>COUNT</code> ignores. The <code>DISTINCT</code> counts each user only once, even if they did the step several times. I multiply by <code>100.0</code> to avoid integer division, which would often give 0.\n\nFor a rigorous calculation, at the second step I count only the users who went through the first, over the same period.","syntaxe":"SELECT\n  100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT\n    CASE WHEN step = 'b'\n    THEN user_id END)\n  / COUNT(DISTINCT\n    CASE WHEN step = 'a'\n    THEN user_id END)\n  AS rate\nFROM events;"},"ent-v2-139":{"q":"How do you identify a user’s activity sessions in SQL, knowing that a new session starts after more than 30 minutes of inactivity?","a":"I use <code>LAG()</code> to get the time of each user’s previous event, in chronological order: <code>LAG(ts) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts)</code>.\n\nIf more than 30 minutes separate two events, a new session starts: I mark 1, otherwise 0, with <code>CASE WHEN</code>. A user’s first event has no previous one, so <code>LAG()</code> returns <code>NULL</code>: I mark 1 as well.\n\nThen I add up these marks with <code>SUM(mark) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts)</code>: each 1 moves the counter forward, which gives each session a number. A <code>GROUP BY</code> on the user and this number gives the start, the end and the length of each session.","syntaxe":"WITH e AS (\n  SELECT u, ts,\n    CASE WHEN ts - LAG(ts)\n      OVER (PARTITION BY u\n        ORDER BY ts)\n      <= 30 THEN 0\n      ELSE 1 END AS is_start\n  FROM t\n)\nSELECT u, ts,\n  SUM(is_start) OVER (\n    PARTITION BY u\n    ORDER BY ts) AS session\nFROM e;"},"ent-v2-140":{"q":"How do you find products that are often bought together?","a":"I join the purchase lines table with itself, on the purchase number: each result is a pair of products bought together. Then I count the pairs with <code>GROUP BY</code>, and sort by frequency.\n\nThe condition “first product < second product” avoids pairing a product with itself, and counting each pair twice.\n\nThe number of pairs grows fast: a purchase of 10 products gives 45. On a large table, I limit myself to one period.","syntaxe":"SELECT a.product_id,\n  b.product_id,\n  COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM lines AS a\nJOIN lines AS b\n  ON b.purchase_id = a.purchase_id\n  AND a.product_id\n    < b.product_id\nGROUP BY 1, 2\nORDER BY n DESC;"},"ent-v2-141":{"q":"How do you use SQL to compare the conversion rate of two versions of an app in an A/B test?","a":"In an A/B test, users are split into two groups, A and B, each seeing a different version of the app.\n\nFor each group, I calculate the share of users who did the targeted action, like signing up or buying. If each user has a column equal to 1 (yes) or 0 (no), the average per group directly gives that rate: <code>AVG()</code> with a <code>GROUP BY</code> on the group.\n\nBefore drawing conclusions, I check that each user is in only one group, and that the gap is big enough not to be due to chance.","syntaxe":"SELECT test_group,\n  COUNT(*) AS n,\n  AVG(converted) AS rate\nFROM ab_test\nGROUP BY test_group;"},"ent-v2-142":{"q":"Calculate the revenue generated by each customer, using only paid orders.","a":"<code>JOIN</code> links the customers table to the orders table, <code>WHERE</code> keeps only the paid orders, and <code>SUM</code> adds up the amounts with a <code>GROUP BY</code> per customer.\n\nI group by the ID and by the name: two customers can have the same name, the ID tells them apart.\n\nIf customers with no paid order must appear with 0, I switch to a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>, move the condition on the status into the <code>ON</code>, and use <code>COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)</code>.","syntaxe":"SELECT c.id, c.last_name,\n  SUM(o.amount) AS revenue\nFROM customers AS c\nJOIN orders AS o\n  ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.status = 'paid'\nGROUP BY c.id, c.last_name;"}}};
